WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.792
Freshness is one of the clearest differences among these objects.

00:00:03.792 --> 00:00:07.612
A later CTE statement uses current source data.

00:00:07.612 --> 00:00:11.512
A standard view reads through to the current underlying data.

00:00:11.512 --> 00:00:16.247
A materialized view stays at its last snapshot until refreshed.

00:00:16.247 --> 00:00:20.727
Consumers should never have to guess which freshness model they are using.

00:00:20.727 --> 00:00:24.912
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:24.912 --> 00:00:30.047
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:30.047 --> 00:00:33.532
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:00:33.532 --> 00:00:37.302
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:00:37.302 --> 00:00:44.142
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:00:44.142 --> 00:00:48.027
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project:

00:00:48.027 --> 00:00:52.329
validate the meaning of the result, not only whether the statement executes.
